Fix a real /dev/shm leak in vLLM's CPU KV-offload connector
(--kv-offloading-size): if SharedOffloadRegion.__init__() fails partway
through mmap()/MADV_POPULATE_WRITE (e.g. OSError EFAULT, which happens
when the backing tmpfs doesn't have room to actually commit the pages a
large region just sparse-allocated via ftruncate — hit on this repo's
16GB /dev/shm with --kv-offloading-size 12 after two restarts), the
creator's multi-GB file was left orphaned in /dev/shm forever. Two such
leaks are enough to fill a 16GB tmpfs outright, at which point every
subsequent start fails the same way even with a fully healthy config.

This happens inside EngineCoreProc.__init__(), before vLLM's own
shutdown()/cleanup() machinery exists to run, so there is no later hook
to catch it. An atexit-based fix was considered and rejected: verified
directly (not assumed) that atexit callbacks registered inside a
multiprocessing child do NOT fire when that child exits via an uncaught
exception (reproduced in isolation: multiprocessing.Process target raises,
child exitcode=1, atexit callback never ran). The fix instead wraps the
risky mmap()+madvise() section in try/except and cleans up inline, in the
same stack frame as the failure, which cannot depend on any exit-handler
machinery at all. Reproduced the exact real failure against the real
kernel (oversized region > tmpfs capacity, same OSError(14, 'Bad address')
as the live crash) and confirmed the file no longer survives it.

Apply from the installed vllm package directory:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/offload-cleanup-on-init-failure.patch

Written against vLLM 0.27.1. Reapply after upgrades.
